    52	Round 1 (earlier the same day, default flash-attention setting, 3 timed passes): v4 completed all 15 timed calls, median 28.8 s end-to-end (26.9 s on the phone). GUI-Owl's and MAI-UI's first calls never completed before their client gave up (MobileWorld's 120 s timeout plus re-sends), so round 2 used one long timeout without re-sends for every model. A single MAI-UI Q4_K_M probe with flash attention took 1176.1 s (1066.2 s on the prompt) and ended in a 2048-token degenerate output (finish reason `length`).
    53	
    54	The phone slowed under sustained load: after about two hours the sampled fastest-core frequency had fallen from 4.03 GHz to about 2.65 GHz and v4's same screenshots went from ~27 s to ~38 s; that is why option 2 runs the models in both orders. Raw requests, thermal samples and server logs: working repo `results/qdc/`.
    55	
    56	Earlier attempt on a Pixel 9 (Tensor G4, Android 16) through Firebase Android Device Streaming, stopped after v4 when we switched to Qualcomm Device Cloud: v4 median 74.4 s/step over 11 calls, rising from 43.1 to 91.2 s (not monotonically; the last call took 66.7 s). From the 5th request (4th timed call) the sampled frequencies of the three performance cores (CPU 4–6, max 2.6 GHz) fell to ~1.8 GHz and the prime core (CPU 7, max 3.1 GHz) slowed and varied (down to 1.4 GHz and lower), while the battery warmed from 27 to ~36 °C and prompt speed fell from ~26 to ~15 tok/s; Android's thermal status still read 0. These are sampled current frequencies, not recorded caps (raw data and thermal log in the working repo, `results/devstream/`).
    57	
    58	
    59	## How it was measured
    60	
    61	* **CPU benchmark.** Host: GCP n2-standard-16 (Intel Xeon @ 2.80 GHz), otherwise idle. Each call is the first step of a
    62	  fresh episode on one of the 5 screenshots with that task's real goal, driven through the evaluation agent code (LFM3
    63	  agent with the v4 prompt; MobileWorld's official GUI-Owl agent; MobileWorld's official MAI-UI agent with one
    64	  screenshot). One warm-up call, then 3 passes. Latency = wall time of `agent.predict()` (prompt building, image
    65	  encoding, HTTP, model, parsing); prompt/output timings come from the server's per-request log.
    66	* **Prompt cache.** This llama.cpp build keeps a RAM prompt cache (`--cache-ram`, 8 GB default), so GUI-Owl's and
    67	  MAI-UI's repeated screenshots in passes 2–3 were served from cache. Only calls whose prompt was actually processed
    68	  are kept (v4 14, GUI-Owl 4, MAI-UI 4; the kept calls are tightly clustered). The cache also inflated GUI-Owl/MAI-UI
    69	  server memory (~7.5 GB RSS), so memory is shown as model file size; v4's server used 2.8 GB.
    70	* **Phones.** The model runs ON the phone: llama.cpp b12110 (official Android arm64 build = `cd959bae9`), CPU,
    71	  `-t 4 -np 1 --cache-ram 0`, the evaluation flags of each model; model files downloaded on the phone and SHA-256-checked
    72	  against the files used everywhere else; the same 5 screenshots and agent code as the CPU benchmark, driven from the
    73	  Mac. Each request went through a recording proxy that wrote the phone-side llama.cpp timings to disk as it
    74	  completed. Order: pass 1 v4 → GUI-Owl → MAI-UI, pass 2 reversed; the first call of each model in each pass is a
    75	  warm-up on a mirrored screenshot and is excluded. Phone-side prompt time counts every prompt token (no prefix reuse on
    76	  the phone), unlike the CPU table where llama.cpp reused the cached system prompt. End-to-end latency includes the
    77	  SSH/adb tunnel to the device lab (~2–3 s).
    78	* **MAI-UI on the M5 runs at Q8_0.** At Q4_K_M MAI-UI-2B's output collapses into empty `<thinking>` loops on
    79	  AndroidWorld screens ([evidence](results/maiui_quant_check.md)); its AndroidWorld arm therefore uses the 8-bit file
    80	  (1.83 GB + 0.45 GB projector), which is also somewhat slower per token than Q4_K_M. Its CPU row above is Q4_K_M
    81	  (on the MobileWorld screenshots used there its output was well-formed).
    82	
    83	## Why v4 is faster
    84	
    85	Prompt processing (including the vision encoder) dominates on CPU: v4 sends ~800 prompt tokens per step (~750 image
    86	tokens) against ~2,570 for GUI-Owl/MAI-UI, and processes them ~3× faster per token on this CPU (149 vs ~47 tok/s; LFM3
    87	is a hybrid convolution/attention model). v4 also writes shorter outputs (28 vs 47/90 tokens) and generates ~2× faster.
    88	The smaller image budget is part of the trade-off: GUI-Owl's higher seed-30 accuracy (23 vs 18) costs ~2.4× (M5 GPU) to
    89	~9× (CPU) more time per step. A phone CPU/NPU will be slower in absolute terms; the ratios are the useful part.
